  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The proposed equation and its high predictive accuracy are based on a road network dataset from a single country, which may limit its direct applicability to regions with vastly different spatial road patterns.",
      "what": "This study developed a method to determine the required sample size for assessing the positional accuracy of linear features in cartographic quality control using Monte Carlo simulations. The method was tested on a database of 46,460 km of roads across 10 Spanish provinces, analyzing evaluation areas ranging from 1 to 250 square kilometers. Using the Kolmogorov-Smirnov test to compare samples against the population, the researchers produced an equation that calculates sample size based on the assessment area, achieving a coefficient of determination of 0.9252.",
      "why": "It establishes a mathematically rigorous, equation-based standard for sampling linear features like roads, filling a gap where sampling guidelines previously only existed for point-based positional accuracy."
    }
  },
